// Chapter 29 — Data views.
//
// Every file and every command in this chapter was run against the `wcl`
// CLI before it was written down. The live examples on the page use the
// components declared below; their `ch29_` prefix keeps the globally
// registered kind names out of the other forty-one chapters' way.

// A callout card driven by three slots. The live instances further down
// the page are the same declaration the first code block shows.
wdoc_component ch29_metric {
  slot label:  utf8
  slot value:  f64
  slot status: utf8 = "note"
  wdoc_body {
    callout $"${label}" { class = [status]  body = $"Currently at **${value}%**" }
  }
}

// A layout wrapper: `wdoc_content` marks where the instance's own nested
// blocks land.
wdoc_component ch29_panel {
  slot title:   utf8
  slot content: content
  wdoc_body {
    h4 $"${title}"
    wdoc_content
  }
}

// The record type the live `type_table` at the end of the chapter reflects.
@block("ch29_server")
type Ch29Server {
  @doc("How the fleet refers to this host.")
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  @doc("What it runs.")
  role: utf8
  @doc("Current utilisation, per cent.")
  cpu: f64
  @doc("A callout class: `note`, `warning` or `error`.")
  status: utf8
  @doc("Prose about this host, rendered wherever a `project` asks for it.")
  @child("body") overview: WdocAddressableBody?
}

page wdoc_data_views {
  title = "Data views"

  h1 "Data views"

  p "A **data view** is document content computed from data rather than typed out block by block. You declare the data once — as a `let` list, or as blocks a `@document` schema gathers — and every card, table, chart, diagram and page you show it in derives from that one declaration. Change the data and every view moves with it."

  p "This chapter covers the seven mechanisms wdoc gives you for that, in the order you meet them: a **component** (a reusable fragment with named slots), a **content slot** (a hole in a component the caller fills with blocks), a **repeater** (render a body once per element), **`wdoc_instance`** (pick the component from data), **`partial` / `collect`** (scatter content, gather it in one place), **`body` / `project`** (attach content to a data record and render it elsewhere), and **`type_table`** (build a table out of a schema). It ends with the comparison — which of the seven to reach for — and with the one naming hazard that silently empties a repeater."

  p "Every file below was run before it was written down. Type them and compare."

  h2 "One worked example"

  p "Start with the whole thing. Save this as `fleet.wcl`. It declares a record type, two records, one component, and a page that renders three views of the same two records:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "fleet.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
# fleet.wcl — one data set, three views of it.
import <wdoc.wcl>

# What a host is. The `@doc` text reaches the generated table at the bottom.
@block("server")
type Server {
  @doc("How the fleet refers to this host.")
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  @doc("What it runs.")                            role:   utf8
  @doc("Current utilisation, per cent.")           cpu:    f64
  @doc("A callout class: note, warning or error.") status: utf8
  @doc("Prose about this host, rendered wherever a `project` asks for it.")
  @child("body") overview: WdocAddressableBody?
}

@document
type Fleet {
  @children("server") servers: list<Server>
}

server web1 {
  role   = "frontend"
  cpu    = 42.0
  status = "note"
  body { p $"Terminates TLS for the estate. Sitting at ${cpu}%." }
}

server db1 {
  role   = "database"
  cpu    = 88.0
  status = "warning"
  body { p $"The primary. Sitting at ${cpu}%." }
}

# One card, written once, stamped per host.
wdoc_component metric {
  slot label:  utf8
  slot value:  f64
  slot status: utf8 = "note"
  wdoc_body {
    callout $"${label}" { class = [status]  body = $"Currently at **${value}%**" }
  }
}

site handbook {
  default_template = :book
  title            = "Fleet"
  toc { chapter "Fleet" { page = fleet } }
}

page fleet {
  title = "Fleet"
  h1 "Fleet"

  wdoc_repeater { each = servers  as = :s
    h2 $"${s.name}"
    project { from = s.overview }
    metric { label = s.role  value = s.cpu  status = s.status }
  }

  h2 "Every host at a glance"

  table {
    header = ["Host", "Role", "CPU %"]
    rows   = map(servers, fn(s: Server) -> list<utf8> [s.name, s.role, $"${s.cpu}"])
  }

  h2 "What a server record holds"

  type_table { type = Server }
}
WCL
  }

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl check fleet.wcl
OK
$ wcl wdoc build fleet.wcl --out _site
wrote 1 page
TEXT
  }

  p "One page comes out, and nothing on it was written twice. The `web1` and `db1` headings, their prose and their cards come from the repeater. The table rows come from `map`. The property table at the bottom comes from the schema. Adding a third `server` block adds a heading, a paragraph, a card and a table row — and changes nothing else in the file."

  p "The rest of the chapter takes that file apart."

  h2 "Components"

  p "A `wdoc_component` is a named fragment of ordinary wdoc markup with named holes in it. Declare it at the **document root** — beside your pages, not inside one — with a `slot` per hole and a `wdoc_body` holding the markup:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
wdoc_component metric {
  slot label:  utf8
  slot value:  f64
  slot status: utf8 = "note"
  wdoc_body {
    callout $"${label}" { class = [status]  body = $"Currently at **${value}%**" }
  }
}
WCL
  }

  p "Instantiate it by writing its **own name** as a block, anywhere a content block is legal. The instance's fields fill the slots:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
metric { label = "CPU"    value = 42.0  status = "warning" }
metric { label = "Memory" value = 12.0 }
WCL
  }

  p "That is the live pair below — the second instance takes the `status` slot's default:"

  ch29_metric { label = "CPU"    value = 42.0  status = "warning" }
  ch29_metric { label = "Memory" value = 12.0 }

  p "A slot value reaches the body two ways. In text, through an interpolated string — `$\"${label}\"`, with the `$` prefix that makes the string interpolating rather than literal ([Values and primitives](lang_values)). In a field, as a bare reference — `class = [status]`. Both are ordinary WCL name resolution: the body is evaluated with the slot names bound in scope."

  h3 "The derived kind schema"

  p "`metric { … }` is not special syntax. `WdocComponent` carries `@declares_kind`, which is the language's word for _instances of this type declare block kinds of their own_. So when you write `wdoc_component metric`, the language derives a `@block(\"metric\")` schema from that instance's slot blocks, and `metric { … }` is checked against it like any other block."

  p "The derivation is mechanical. One field per slot, in declaration order. A slot carrying a `?` or a default is optional; every other slot is listed in the derived `required_fields`. So the two ordinary block errors both fire on a component instance:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl check fleet.wcl        # with `metric { label = "Memory" }`
wcl::eval::schema_violation

  × block 'metric' is missing required field 'value'

fleet.wcl: 1 schema violation

$ wcl check fleet.wcl        # with `colour = "red"` added to an instance
wcl::eval::schema_violation

  × field 'colour' is not declared by schema 'metric'

fleet.wcl: 1 schema violation
TEXT
  }

  p "There is no component vocabulary inside the language, and no hand-rolled slot checker. An unfilled slot is a missing required field; a misspelt slot is an unknown field. See [Schemas](lang_schemas) for the two errors in their ordinary setting."

  callout "A slot's declared type is not checked" {
    class = ["warning"]
    body  = "Membership and required-ness are the whole of the check on a component instance. The derived schema stops there: `value = \"twelve\"` against `slot value: f64` passes `wcl check` today, where the same value in an ordinary `cpu: f64` field fails with `field 'cpu' declared as f64 but value is utf8`. Annotate your slots anyway — the type says what the slot is for, and it is what makes `?` mean optional — but do not expect the tool to hold you to it."
  }

  h3 "slot and wdoc_slot"

  p "There are two spellings of a slot declaration, and the newer one is the typed one:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Written as"                | "Means" |
      | "`slot label: utf8`"        | "Required. The type annotation is retained." |
      | "`slot label: utf8?`"       | "Optional. Absent at the instance, the slot is `none`." |
      | "`slot status: utf8 = \"note\"`" | "Optional, with a fallback the body sees when the instance omits it." |
      | "`slot content: content`"   | "A **content hole**, not a parameter — see [Content slots](#content-slots)." |
      | "`wdoc_slot label`"         | "The older untyped form. Required." |
      | "`wdoc_slot status { default = \"note\" }`" | "The older form's fallback, written as a field." |
  }

  p "Both spellings may appear in one component, and one derivation reads both. An untyped `wdoc_slot` derives a permissive `@schemaless utf8` field, which is the compatibility older documents rely on. Reach for `slot` in new work."

  h3 "Content slots"

  p "A slot whose type is `content` is a different animal: it is a hole for **blocks**, not for a value. It derives no field on the instance schema at all. Instead the instance fills it by nesting blocks, and the body renders them."

  p "The simplest case has one hole. `wdoc_content` in the body marks where the instance's own nested blocks land:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
wdoc_component panel {
  slot title:   utf8
  slot content: content
  wdoc_body {
    h4 $"${title}"
    wdoc_content          # the caller's nested blocks render here
  }
}

panel { title = "Notes"
  p "Anything nested in the instance renders at wdoc_content."
  list {
    li "including lists"
    li "and more"
  }
}
WCL
  }

  p "Live:"

  ch29_panel { title = "Notes"
    p "Anything nested in the instance renders at `wdoc_content`."
    list {
      li "including lists"
      li "and more"
    }
  }

  p "`content` is the reserved name for the loose children — every nested block the instance does not put in a named hole goes there, and `wdoc_content` is the marker that renders it. A component with **several** holes names them, and each is both filled and rendered by its own name:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
wdoc_component splitcard {
  slot title: utf8
  slot left:  content
  slot right: content?
  wdoc_body {
    h4 $"${title}"
    left                  # renders whatever the instance's `left { … }` holds
    right
  }
}

splitcard { title = "Two holes"
  left  { p "LEFT" }
  right { p "RIGHT" }
}

splitcard { title = "One hole"
  left { p "ONLY LEFT" }   # `right` is optional, so this is legal
}
WCL
  }

  p "A content hole is not a field, so `wcl check` has nothing to miss. Drop the `left { … }` fill from the last instance above and `check` still prints `OK`; the **build** is what refuses it, naming both the component and the slot:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl check splitcard.wcl
OK
$ wcl wdoc build splitcard.wcl --out _site
component `splitcard`: required slot `left` is unfilled
$ echo $?
3
TEXT
  }

  p "Scalar slots are the schema's business; content slots are the renderer's."

  h2 "Repeating over data"

  p "A `wdoc_repeater` renders its body once per element of the list its `each` field evaluates to, binding each element to the symbol its `as` field names:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
wdoc_repeater { each = servers  as = :s
  h2 $"${s.name}"
  p  $"${s.role}, at ${s.cpu}%"
}
WCL
  }

  p "The binding is an ordinary name in scope for the whole body, so it reaches every field of every block inside — including a nested component instance, which is how the worked example stamps one card per host:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
wdoc_repeater { each = servers  as = :s
  metric { label = s.role  value = s.cpu  status = s.status }
}
WCL
  }

  p "`as` is optional; without it the binding is called `it`. Bindings **stack**, so a repeater inside a component body sees both the loop variable and the enclosing component's slots — which is how a component takes a whole list through one slot and names the layout for it:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
wdoc_component fleet_panel {
  slot heading: utf8
  slot rows:    list<Server>
  wdoc_body {
    h3 $"${heading}"
    wdoc_repeater { each = rows  as = :s
      metric { label = s.role  value = s.cpu  status = s.status }
    }
  }
}

fleet_panel { heading = "Fleet"  rows = servers }
WCL
  }

  p "Where the data comes from is not the repeater's business. A `let` list of records, a `@children` gather field, the result of `map` or `filter` over either — anything that evaluates to a list will do. See [Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections) and [Functions](lang_functions)."

  h3 "What each does and does not report"

  p "The two failure modes differ, and the difference is deliberate. An `each` expression that **fails to evaluate** is a real error, and the build says so with a snippet:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl wdoc build fleet.wcl --out _site
wcl::eval::unresolved_reference

  × unresolved reference 'inventory'
   ╭─[fleet.wcl:6:26]
 5 │   h1 "Fleet"
 6 │   wdoc_repeater { each = inventory  as = :h
   ·                          ────┬────
   ·                              ╰── does not resolve
   ╰────
TEXT
  }

  p "An `each` that evaluates to something that is **not a list** — a number, a record, `none` — renders nothing, silently. So does an empty list, which is the point: a repeater over no data is not an error. If a repeater you expected to produce rows produces none and the build stays green, its `each` resolved to a non-list. [The gather-field name collision](#the-gather-field-name-collision) is the usual reason."

  h3 "A repeater is the one iteration concept"

  p "The same block repeats at every level of a document, because every container that walks its children expands repeaters first. Four placements are worth knowing by name:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Written inside"     | "Body holds"        | "Produces" |
      | "A `page`"           | "Content blocks"    | "Repeated page content" |
      | "A `diagram` or `container`" | "Diagram shapes" | "One shape per element — see [The diagram canvas](wdoc_diagrams)" |
      | "The **document root**" | "`page` blocks"  | "One rendered page per element; the page's interpolated label is its route" |
      | "A `toc` or `chapter`" | "`chapter` blocks" | "One navigation entry per element" |
  }

  p "The last two together give a whole catalogue site with no hand-written page or chapter boilerplate:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
let containers = [
  { id: "web", name: "Web App" },
  { id: "api", name: "API" },
  { id: "db",  name: "Database" },
]

# At the document root: one rendered page per element.
wdoc_repeater { each = containers  as = :c
  page $"cont_${c.id}" {
    title = c.name
    h1 $"${c.name}"
  }
}

site catalogue {
  default_template = :book
  toc {
    chapter "Containers" {
      # Inside the TOC: one entry per element, naming the generated page.
      wdoc_repeater { each = containers  as = :c
        chapter $"${c.name}" { page = $"cont_${c.id}" }
      }
    }
  }
}
WCL
  }

  callout "A generated route is a slug" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "The page's interpolated label becomes its route, so it must be non-empty, hold only `A-Za-z0-9_-`, and be unique within its site. A colliding or space-bearing route fails the build. Build one from prose with `to_lower(replace(name, \" \", \"-\"))`. Links to a generated page resolve normally: the pages exist before links are checked, so a link whose target is computed from the same data works."
  }

  p "A repeater also composes with itself and with components in any order: a repeater inside a component body, a component instance inside a repeater body, a repeater inside a repeater. Diagram shapes are the case where this pays off most — see [Connections and routing](wdoc_diagram_connections) for a graph whose nodes and edges both come from one list."

  h2 "Choosing the component from data"

  p "Writing a component's name as a block fixes the choice in the source. `wdoc_instance` instead renders the component named by the **value** of its `component` field, so each element of a list can pick its own:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
let widgets = [
  { kind: "badge", text: "one" },
  { kind: "pill",  text: "two" },
]

wdoc_repeater { each = widgets  as = :w
  wdoc_instance { component = w.kind  text = w.text }
}
WCL
  }

  p "The instance's like-named fields fill the target's slots, falling back to each slot's `default` exactly as a direct instantiation does. `wdoc_instance` is `@schemaless`, so the forwarded fields need no schema of their own — which also means none of them are checked. A `component` value naming no declared component renders nothing."

  h2 "Scatter and collect"

  p "Components and repeaters both assemble content **at one spot**. A `partial` does the opposite: it deposits tagged content wherever you happen to be writing, and a `collect` with the same tag gathers every deposit in the document and renders them, in document order, somewhere else. It is the appendix, the glossary, the collected-sidebars pattern."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
partial aside { callout "From section one" { body = "A point to collect later." } }

# ... prose, other blocks, other pages, other imported files ...

partial aside { callout "From section two" { body = "Another point." } }

# Gather every `aside` deposit here, in document order:
collect aside
WCL
  }

  p "A deposit is **invisible where it is written**. Set `show_here = true` to render it in place as well as at the collect site:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
partial aside { show_here = true  p "Pinned in place and also collected." }
WCL
  }

  p "Two `partial ch29_aside` deposits sit in this page's source immediately below this paragraph, and neither of them renders there. The `collect` that follows them picks up both:"

  partial ch29_aside { callout "From the first deposit" { class = ["note"]  body = "Written where the prose needed it." } }

  partial ch29_aside { callout "From the second deposit" { class = ["tip"]  body = "Written somewhere else entirely." } }

  collect ch29_aside

  callout "What a collect reaches" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "Collection is **document-global**, not page-local: a `collect` gathers matching deposits from the root document and every file a top-level `import` pulls in, whichever page each one sits on. Tag distinctly, or one page's sidebars turn up in another's appendix. Three limits: a file brought in by a block-scoped (lazy) `import` is not reached; a `partial` nested inside another partial's body is part of that body and is not collected separately; and a collected body should carry no `id`s, because per-page id checks run before collection. A `collect` whose gathered content holds a `collect` of the same tag renders nothing for the inner one — the cycle is broken rather than followed."
  }

  h2 "Content that rides on a record"

  p "A `partial` addresses content by **tag**. That is the right handle when the content is a loose deposit and the gathering site is a fixed place in the document. It is the wrong handle when the content belongs to a **record** — when each server, each tutorial step, each API endpoint carries its own prose, and the prose has to travel with the record wherever the record is rendered."

  p "That is what `body` and `project` are for. A `body` is a chunk of renderable content attached to a data record as a property. A `project` renders the body its `from` field resolves to."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("server")
type Server {
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  cpu: f64
  @child("body") overview: WdocAddressableBody?   # content rides on the record
}

server web1 {
  cpu = 42.0
  body { p $"Terminates TLS. Sitting at ${cpu}%." }
}

page fleet {
  wdoc_repeater { each = servers  as = :s
    h2 $"${s.name}"
    project { from = s.overview }                 # render THIS record's body
  }
}
WCL
  }

  p "Three things about that are worth spelling out."

  list {
    li "**A `body` never renders where it is written.** It is not a content block, so a page renderer walks straight past it. The only way to its contents is a `project`."
    li "**`${…}` inside a body resolves against the record it hangs on.** `${cpu}` above is `web1`'s `cpu`, and the rendered line reads _Terminates TLS. Sitting at 42%._ You are not copying the record's fields into the prose by hand."
    li "**`from` carries an address, not a copy.** `body` is declared `@by_ref`, so when the repeater reads a server record as data, the record's `overview` slot reifies to a reference rather than to inlined content. `s.overview` is therefore the address of _that_ server's fragment, and one line of `project` renders a different body per element."
  }

  h3 "Addressing a body"

  p "How you name a body depends on how it is attached."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Attached as"                         | "Addressed by"                    | "Write the body as" |
      | "A single `@child(\"body\")` slot"     | "The slot: `from = s.overview`"   | "`body { … }`" |
      | "A `@children(\"body\")` list"         | "The slot: `from = t.notes`, which renders every body in the list, in order" | "`body intro { … }`" |
      | "Standalone at the document root"      | "Its label, through the root's `bodies` field" | "`body glossary { … }`" |
  }

  p "A `body` in a list, or one standing alone at the root, needs the `@inline(0)` label — that is what the path segment matches. A `body` filling a single `@child` slot is already named by the slot, so its label may be dropped."

  p "A `project` written at page scope — outside any repeater, with nothing bound — names the full path from a root gather field instead:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
project { from = tutorials.deploy.steps.1.detail }
WCL
  }

  p "Records carrying a body nest to any depth, and a numeric `@inline(0)` label is fine. Read that path as [Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents) reads any other: `tutorials` is the root gather field, `deploy` and `1` are labels, `steps` is a nested gather field, `detail` is the body slot."

  callout "steps.1 is a label, not an index" {
    class = ["warning"]
    body  = "`steps.1` matches the step **labelled** `1`. It is not the first element of the list. Relabel the steps `10`, `20`, `30` and `steps.1` resolves to nothing, while `steps.10` resolves to the first one. Path segments address blocks by label everywhere in WCL; a body slot is no exception."
  }

  h3 "When a project has nothing to render"

  p "`from` must evaluate to a body reference. Anything else is a build error naming the block:"

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl wdoc build fleet.wcl --out _site
wcl::eval::user_error

  × error: `project`'s `from` did not resolve to a body reference; it must
  │ name an addressable `body` (e.g. a `@by_ref` property of the data being
  │ generated from)
   ╭─[fleet.wcl:6:3]
 6 │   project { from = "nope" }
   ·   ────────────┬────────────
   ·               ╰── error raised here
   ╰────
TEXT
  }

  p "A `from` that resolves to a path holding something other than a body reports the path it followed instead — _project target tutorials.deploy did not resolve to a body_. And a body that projects itself terminates rather than recursing: the inner projection renders nothing and the outer one completes."

  h2 "Tables from the schema"

  p "The last data view takes its data from the **schema** rather than from records. `type_table { type = T }` reflects a type's fields into a documentation table, so a reference page cannot drift from the declaration it documents."

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("server")
type Server {
  @doc("How the fleet refers to this host.")
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  @doc("What it runs.") role: utf8
  @hidden internal_seed: u32?
  @child("body") overview: WdocAddressableBody?
}

page reference {
  type_table { type = Server }
}
WCL
  }

  p "Reflecting this chapter's own `Ch29Server` type gives:"

  type_table { type = Ch29Server }

  p "Two tables come out. The first lists the scalar properties — name, type, whether the field is required, and the description. The second, headed **Child blocks**, lists the type's `@child` / `@children` slots with what each accepts and whether it holds one block or a list; it is omitted entirely when the type has no block slots. Fields inherited through `extends` are included, own fields first."

  p "Two decorators steer the output, and both are authored on the **schema**, not on the table:"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Decorator"       | "Effect" |
      | "`@doc(\"…\")`"    | "The description column. A language built-in — see [Decorators](lang_decorators)." |
      | "`@hidden`"       | "Drops the field from the generated table. Declared by wdoc." |
  }

  p "Function-typed fields are dropped automatically, which is what keeps every block type's `lower` hook out of its own reference table. See [Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending) for what `lower` is."

  h3 "Reflecting a whole document"

  p "`block_reference { type = MyDoc }` walks a `@document` type's block slots and emits an `h3` plus a `type_table` for each — so a document schema documents its own top-level tags with no hand-maintained list:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
import <wdoc.wcl>

@document
type MyDoc {
  @children("widget") widgets:  list<Widget>
  @child("settings")  settings: Settings
}

@block("widget")   type Widget   { @inline(0) id: identifier  @doc("Pixels wide.") width: u32 }
@block("settings") type Settings { @doc("UI theme") theme: utf8 }

page reference {
  block_reference { type = MyDoc }   # one heading + property table per block
}
WCL
  }

  p "Both components are thin wrappers over reflection builtins — `type_fields` for the field list, `child_types` for a type's block-slot element types, `decorator_arg` for the `@block(\"…\")` kind name that becomes each heading. See [Builtins](lang_builtins). Drop to the repeater directly when you want a different layout:"

  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
wdoc_repeater { each = child_types(MyDoc)  as = :b
  type_table { type = b }
}
WCL
  }

  callout "A union or interface slot documents as one name" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "`child_types` resolves a slot to its declared element type. A slot that accepts a union or an interface resolves to that type's name, and `type_table` documents a single concrete type, so such a slot is not expanded into a table per member. Document the members individually."
  }

  h2 "The gather-field name collision"

  p "Here is the one hazard that will cost you an afternoon. Every data view above reads a gather field, and the names a data-view author reaches for are exactly the names wdoc's own `@document` schema already uses."

  p "`@document` schemas **compose per namespace**: importing `<wdoc.wcl>` and declaring your own root `@document` is not a conflict, it is a merge, and that is what lets you add top-level tags of your own ([Schemas](lang_schemas)). But the merged schema is one flat name space, and a gather field declared on both sides resolves to only one of the two declarations."

  code wcl {
    filename = "clash.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
import <wdoc.wcl>

@document
type App {
  @children("component") pages: list<Component>   # `pages` is already wdoc's
}

@block("component")
type Component { @inline(0) id: identifier  name: utf8 }

component web { name = "Web" }
component api { name = "API" }

site s { default_template = :book  title = "T"  toc { chapter "C" { page = c } } }

page c {
  title = "Components"
  h1 "Components"
  wdoc_repeater { each = pages  as = :x
    h2 $"${x.name}"
  }
}
WCL
  }

  code text {
    source = <<'TEXT'
$ wcl check clash.wcl
warning: gather field 'pages' of @document 'App' (this document) collides with
'pages' declared by @document 'wdoc.Site' (<wcl-system>/wdoc/core.wcl) — the
merged document schema resolves 'pages' to only one declaration, so the other
schema's gathered blocks silently vanish; rename one field
clash.wcl: 1 warning
OK

$ wcl wdoc build clash.wcl --out _site
wrote 1 page
TEXT
  }

  p "The build is green and the page is empty. Two `component` blocks are in the file; the repeater rendered nothing at all, because `each = pages` resolved to the other declaration. Nothing failed — the check is a **warning**, and the repeater's silent-on-a-non-list rule swallowed the rest."

  p "The names to avoid are the ones wdoc's `@document` already gathers: `pages`, `sites`, `components`, `partials`, `bodies`, `generators`, `styles`, `themes`, `templates`, `patterns`, `iconsets`, `tilesets`. Notice how many of them are the natural name for a data-view gather. The fix is one word — name yours `sw_components`, `catalog_pages`, `service_bodies`. Do not ignore the warning."

  h2 "Choosing a mechanism"

  p "Seven tools, and the choice between them is nearly always decided by one question: **where does the content live, and what addresses it?**"

  table {
    rows:
      | "Mechanism"          | "Content lives"                  | "Addressed by"          | "Reach for it when" |
      | "`wdoc_component`"   | "In the component's `wdoc_body`" | "The component's name"  | "One layout, many call sites with different values" |
      | "`wdoc_content` / a `content` slot" | "At the call site" | "The slot's name"       | "The component frames content the caller writes" |
      | "`wdoc_repeater`"    | "In the repeater's body"         | "Nothing — it is written where it renders" | "One body, once per element of a list" |
      | "`wdoc_instance`"    | "In some component's body"       | "A **value**"           | "The data chooses which component renders it" |
      | "`partial` / `collect`" | "Wherever you wrote the deposit" | "A **tag**"          | "Content is scattered; one place assembles it" |
      | "`body` / `project`" | "Inside a data record"           | "A **reference** to that record's slot" | "Prose belongs to a record and travels with it" |
      | "`type_table`"       | "In the schema"                  | "A **type**"            | "The table is the declaration, restated" |
  }

  p "Three of those pairs look alike and are not:"

  list {
    li "**`wdoc_instance` against a component instance.** Both render a component. A component instance names it in the source; `wdoc_instance` names it with a value. Use the plain form until an element of your data has to decide."
    li "**`partial` / `collect` against `body` / `project`.** Both write content in one place and render it in another. A partial is addressed by a tag, so many deposits share one name and the collect site takes all of them. A body is addressed by a reference to the record that owns it, so one `project` inside a repeater renders a different fragment per element. If the content belongs to a record, it wants a `body`; if it belongs to a stretch of the narrative, it wants a `partial`."
    li "**A component with a list slot against a repeater.** A repeater iterates. A component with a `list`-typed slot iterates _and_ names the layout, so the call site reads `fleet_panel { rows = servers }` instead of a loop. Reach for the component the second time you write the same loop."
  }

  p "The mechanisms compose without ceremony, because they are all block expansion. A repeater inside a component body inside a repeater is ordinary. A `project` inside a component body inside a diagram is ordinary. Anything that walks a container's children expands generators first, so a generated block participates exactly as an authored one does."

  h2 "Where to go next"

  list {
    li "[Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections) — the list values a repeater iterates, and `map` / `filter` / `flatten` for shaping them."
    li "[Schemas](lang_schemas) — `@document` merging, gather fields, and the collision warning above in its own setting."
    li "[Decorators](lang_decorators) — `@doc`, and what a decorator is before wdoc adds `@hidden`."
    li "[Builtins](lang_builtins) — `type_fields`, `child_types`, `decorator_arg` and the rest of the reflection surface."
    li "[Lists and tables](wdoc_tables) — a `table`'s `header` / `rows` fields, which is where a computed table's cells land."
    li "[Charts](wdoc_charts) — feeding `map`ped data straight into a chart's value fields."
    li "[The diagram canvas](wdoc_diagrams) and [Connections and routing](wdoc_diagram_connections) — repeater-generated shapes and computed `edges`."
    li "[Writing your own blocks](wdoc_extending) — when a component is not enough and you want a block kind of your own."
  }
}
